:NETTP2WFBINB
echo Because the Wi-Fi is bad, the internet is bad
echo.
echo Even if you go to speedtest.net and see a low download and upload speed, if it is just on regular Wi-Fi, it is you that is the problem.
echo Depending on your setup, you need to either
echo - switch to Ethernet (best solution)
echo or
echo - stand really close to the router
echo Because, just putting your router next to your computer can certianly increase to almost double the speed.
echo And, if you have an Ethernet port and you have a long enough cable, it gives you lower latency and an extra double the speed.
echo If you don't have an Ethernet port or you are really far away from your router and you cannot change its position, the only option
echo you have, is a MESH system. Just plug in some bad boys into the power and put them in the most optimal positions, you may get the
echo same results as if you moved the router next to your computer. But, their problem, is that they can be expensive.
echo It is not the Wi-Fi you got is bad, it is the way the Wi-Fi is.
echo.
echo Another thing you could do, if you want to get the performance of Ethernet, just get your phone, turn "USB Tethering" on, connect
echo your phone to your computer via the longest, fastest cable and put the phone as close to the router. Especially good for PCs without a
echo Ethernet port or your PC doesn't support Ethernet (why should it not?)
pause
GOTO NETTIPS2
GOTO :EOF

:NETTP2DNSSPD
echo DNS Reliability may impact speed
echo.
echo Depending on the DNS, your internet may be reduced. Exsample, the DNS from your ISP, if it works it really works.
echo If it doesn't work it really doesn't work. And, that is bad. Just setting the DNS using ist --deadzone-set-dns can fix most internet problems.
echo Especially if you set a DNS that doesn't work (like 33456.3456.21.12) that DNS, does not even exist!
pause
GOTO NETTIPS2
GOTO :EOF

:NETTP224GHZ
echo 2.4GHz VS. 5GHz
echo.
echo Both those have their pros and cons. Choose 2.4GHz if
echo - you want your internet to work on any device
echo - a bigger range than 5GHz
echo - if you only connect to the internet on a maximum of 3 devices
echo The problems of 2.4GHz are
echo - are a pain to use with more than 3 devices
echo - slower than 5GHz (a 125mbps limit)
echo - lower efficiency
echo Choose 5GHz if
echo - you have a device that is from 2013 and up
echo - you want more reliability
echo - you want that 100mbps limit gone
echo But nothing is perfect, 5GHz has also problems
echo - is not supported from devices older than 2013 (because of its huge speed difference from 2.4GHz)
echo - is way shorter (may require being way nearer)
echo - don't have thick walls between your connection
pause
GOTO NETTIPS2
GOTO :EOF

:NETTP2MDRO
echo Most people say that the "blinking box of doom" (the thing behind your TV) a Modem and a Router the 
echo same thing, but they have a giant difference between eachother.
echo A Modem:
echo - Brings your Internet into your house
echo - Translates the signal from your ISP
echo - Makes the internet actually work
echo A Router:
echo - Is the thing you actually connect to
echo - Makes the actual LAN
echo - That is the culprit for a bad Internet connection
echo They have huge differences, they are not the same.
pause 
GOTO NETTIPS2
GOTO :EOF

:NETTP2RTFA
echo Sometimes, you are just gaming on Wi-Fi when, it just randomly got worse. Why? It's because of the router. Always try this.
echo If your connection was good before the "incedent", you can try these steps:
echo - Try going next to it (biggest culprit especially for 5GHz)
echo - Try using Ethernet in that same router
echo - Make a Wi-Fi hotspot for your phone.
echo If all these are not true (except the hotspot), a restart may be required.
echo Here's on how to check. Open your web browser and type in the IP of your router. (usually 192.168.178.1)
echo Then, login and find your Uptime. If the Uptime is bigger than 125 days, a restart is recommended.
echo Because:
echo - Updates are in the background, praying for someone to restart the router
echo - Memory leaks
echo - The performance will be reduced
echo Just take your time, unplug the power supply of the router, wait 1-2 minutes, then plug it back in.
echo You may have made your son lose bedwars, but it will jump that speed all the way back as it's supposed to.
echo Also, if your Internet is used a lot (like you have iPad kids), try doing this in the night when everyone
echo is sleeping. So, that your son will not cry for 10 hours straight, just because of a restart. 
pause
GOTO NETTIPS2
